  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.4 × loss, with contour lines. Along x it is 3 times steeper, so steps overshoot there for learning rates above 0.33 and swings grow above 0.67.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64 (the whole simulated data set).",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step and the grey arrow the full-data step (−learning rate × true slope). Both are drawn at 2× their true length. The step-length readout gives the true length and is 0 once a run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged as soon as the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Positions off the bowl are drawn at its edge, but the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence. A new run takes the next values in it. Reset replays the sequence from the start and clears both paths.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches, a single step can raise it, and the ball wanders near the bottom.",
    "Two settings, one minimum, no momentum, no schedule, no Adam. Real losses have many settings, local minima and saddle points."
  ]
}